--- Findings ---

1. Does reducing noise improve stability?
   YES (partially). Lower-noise conditions reduce learning-gain std (baseline=0.0929, half=0.1070, none=0.0871).

2. Does reducing noise hurt generalization?
   YES (tradeoff). Mean learning gain drops when noise is reduced (baseline=0.5634, half=0.5222, none=0.5520). Some transition stochasticity may help exploration of robust policies.

3. Best performance-stability tradeoff:
   Baseline Noise (A) - balances mean LG (0.5634), seed std (0.0929), and action diversity (entropy=1.2782).
